===Verb===
{{de-verb|haben<irreg>}}

# {{lb|de|auxiliary}} {{n-g|forms the [[perfect]] [[aspect]]}} ([[have]]) {{+obj|de|past&part}}
#: {{uxi|de|Das '''habe''' ich nicht gesagt.|I '''have'''n't said that.}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[have]]; to [[own]] {{gloss|to possess, have ownership of; to possess a certain characteristic}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[have]]; to [[hold]] {{gloss|to contain within itself/oneself}}
#: {{ux|de|Glaub und '''hab''' keine Angst.|Believe and don't be afraid ''or'' Believe and '''have''' no fear.}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[have]], [[get]] {{gloss|to obtain, acquire}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[get]] {{gloss|to receive}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[have]] {{gloss|to be scheduled to attend}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[have]] {{gloss|to be afflicted with, suffer from}}
# {{lb|de|transitive|of units of measure}} to [[contain]], be [[compose]]d of, [[equal]]
#: {{ux|de|Ein Meter '''hat''' 100 Zentimeter.|There are 100 centimetres in one metre.|lit=One metre '''has''' 100 centimetres.}}
# {{lb|de|impersonal|regional|with {{m|de|es}}}} [[there be]], [[there is]], [[there are]]
#: {{ux|de|Es '''hat''' zwei Bücher.|There are two books.}}
# {{lb|de|reflexive|colloquial}} to [[make]] a [[fuss]]
#: {{ux|de|'''Hab''' dich nicht so!|Don't make such a fuss!}}
# {{lb|de|colloquial}} to be [[occupied]] with, to [[like]], to be into {{+obj|de|:es + :mit(dat)<something/someone>}}
#: {{ux|de|Ich '''hab''''s nich so mit Hunden.|I'm not a great fan of dogs.|lit=I don't '''have''' it that much with dogs.}}
# {{lb|de|regional|colloquial}} to [[talk]] {{+obj|de|:es + :von(dat)/:über(acc)<about someone/something>}}
#: {{ux|de|Wir '''hatten''''s grad von dir und deiner Freundin.|We've just been talking about you and your girlfriend.|lit=We just '''had''' it about you and your girlfriend.}}
# to [[have]] to; [[must]] {{+obj|de|:zu(inf)<do something>}}
#: {{ux|de|Er '''hat''' sich zu benehmen.|He has to behave himself.}}

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj|haben<irreg>}}
Colloquially, {{m|de|hab}} (also written {{m|de|hab'}}) is often used as the first person singular of the present indicative, instead of {{m|de|habe}}.

